"""Original mixed timed paper; first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
A learner feels supported by friends and included in school activities. This most directly illustrates|social wellbeing|bone structure|pulse rate|joint flexibility|Belonging and supportive relationships are aspects of social wellbeing.
A school teaches hygiene but lacks soap. The missing element is|access to a practical resource|knowledge about sports fixtures|a medical diagnosis|a bone label|Facilities and supplies are needed to put knowledge into practice.
A person sees a claim that a product cures every disease. The best response is to|check reliable evidence and seek qualified advice|stop prescribed care immediately|accept the claim without evidence|share the claim as a diagnosis|Universal cure claims need careful evaluation and do not replace professional advice.
A food supplies amino acids used in tissue repair. Its relevant nutrient is|protein|water alone|dietary fibre alone|iodine alone|Protein supplies amino acids used in growth and tissue maintenance.
A label lists 7 g per serving and three servings per packet. The whole packet contains|21 g|7 g|10 g|28 g|Seven grams per serving multiplied by three servings gives 21 g.
A cook uses an unwashed board for raw meat and salad. The concern is|cross-contamination|safe separation|adequate refrigeration alone|a balanced serving size|Microorganisms can transfer from raw meat to ready-to-eat food via unwashed equipment.
A medicine prescribed to another person should not be assumed safe because|individual conditions, doses and interactions differ|similar symptoms always have one cause|all doses are identical|prescription labels diagnose everyone|Individual circumstances and treatment requirements differ.
A learner asks whether antibiotics cure the common cold. The accurate answer is|no, the common cold is viral and antibiotics do not treat viruses|yes, antibiotics treat all viruses|yes, all colds are bacterial|no, antibiotics never treat bacterial infection|Antibiotics work against susceptible bacteria, not viruses causing common colds.
A person is unresponsive with abnormal breathing after possible substance exposure. A bystander should|call emergency services and follow dispatcher instructions|force the person to drink|wait until morning without monitoring|leave without seeking help|Unresponsiveness and abnormal breathing are emergencies requiring urgent help.
Smoke builds up in a poorly ventilated kitchen. This can increase|exposure to harmful air pollution|certainty that every occupant has the same illness|access to safe drinking water|the number of available health workers|Accumulated smoke can expose occupants to harmful pollutants.
Water looks clear after a flood. Which conclusion is sound?|It may still contain harmful organisms or chemicals|It is guaranteed safe to drink|It cannot become contaminated during storage|Boiling always removes every chemical|Appearance does not establish water safety, and boiling does not remove all chemical contamination.
A community builds toilets but does not maintain or safely empty them. The missing consideration is|the sanitation service chain and maintenance|only the colour of signs|a sporting fixture|an individual's blood group|Sanitation includes safe containment, maintenance, treatment and disposal.
The agent responsible for malaria is|a Plasmodium parasite|all mosquitoes themselves|a dietary mineral|a fracture|Malaria is caused by Plasmodium parasites and commonly transmitted by infected female Anopheles mosquitoes.
A prevention measure aimed at reducing malaria transmission is|appropriate use of an insecticide-treated net|sharing a prescription|assuming fever always confirms malaria|using a net to purify drinking water|Treated nets help reduce mosquito bites and malaria risk when used appropriately.
HIV is not transmitted by|ordinary hugging and sharing food|some contaminated blood exposures|certain sexual exposures|some parent-to-baby transmission|HIV is not spread by ordinary social contact such as hugging and sharing food.
Infectious pulmonary TB can spread|through the air|only by mosquito bite|only through inherited genes|only through a sports whistle|Infectious pulmonary TB can spread through airborne particles.
Which example is generally noncommunicable?|diabetes|malaria|measles|tuberculosis|Diabetes is an NCD; the others are infectious diseases.
A risk factor is associated with a condition. This means|the probability may change but the outcome is not guaranteed|every exposed person has the condition|all other causes are excluded|a diagnosis has been confirmed|Risk concerns probability and does not establish certainty or diagnosis.
A friend reports persistent distress that affects daily life. An appropriate response is to|listen and help them find suitable support|ridicule them|diagnose them from one conversation|promise one activity will cure everything|Respectful listening and access to suitable support can help.
Sudden one-sided weakness and difficulty speaking can|require emergency assessment|always wait several days|be diagnosed by a friend alone|prove a harmless condition|Sudden neurological symptoms can indicate an emergency.
Before assisting at a road accident, a bystander should|check scene safety and call for help|enter moving traffic without checking|ignore hazards|move everyone regardless of necessity|Scene safety protects the responder while urgent help is arranged.
Severe external bleeding without an embedded object may be managed initially by|firm direct pressure when safe and appropriate while calling for help|forcing the person to exercise|ignoring the wound|waiting without monitoring|Firm direct pressure can help control severe bleeding while emergency help is arranged.
For a thermal burn, an appropriate general response is to|cool the burn with cool running water|apply ice directly|pull off material stuck to skin|apply butter as first aid|Cool running water is recommended; avoid ice and do not remove stuck material.
Why should a responder not force a suspected fracture straight?|Unnecessary movement can worsen injury|Straightening always controls bleeding|The limb must be exercised before assessment|The cause is established by appearance alone|Keep the injured area still and seek appropriate assessment; do not force alignment.
An unresponsive person with abnormal breathing needs|emergency help and dispatcher-guided CPR|food forced into the mouth|no monitoring|a delayed textbook diagnosis|Call emergency services and follow dispatcher instructions for CPR and AED use.
A person changes their mind after agreeing to an activity. The other person should|stop and respect the withdrawal of consent|continue because agreement was previously given|assume silence now means agreement|apply pressure until agreement returns|Consent is voluntary and can be withdrawn.
Why can condoms not eliminate every STI risk?|Some infections can spread through skin not covered by a condom|Condoms cure all infections before exposure|All STIs are spread only through mosquito bites|Protection means testing is never useful|Condoms reduce many STI risks, but may not cover all exposed skin.
What should be considered separately when discussing a contraceptive method?|Its pregnancy-prevention role and whether it reduces STI transmission|Only the colour of the packaging|Whether it replaces the need for consent|Whether it proves infection status from appearance|Many contraceptives prevent pregnancy but do not protect against STIs; discuss the two questions separately.
Puberty|varies in timing and experience between people|begins on the same date for everyone|always indicates illness|never involves physical changes|Development and timing vary among individuals.
A person experiencing unwanted sexual contact|can seek trusted support without being blamed|is responsible for another person's coercion|must be ridiculed|should be denied care|Unwanted contact is not the affected person's fault; support and safety are important.
A school health programme is best evaluated by checking|whether facilities work, are accessible and remain supplied|only the number of posters|only the paint colour|only a meeting date|Function, accessibility and maintenance help show whether an intervention is usable.
A hand sanitizer used when soap and water are unavailable should contain at least|60% alcohol|6% alcohol|16% alcohol|40% alcohol|CDC guidance recommends sanitizer containing at least 60% alcohol when soap and water are unavailable.
A mosquito is called a vector when it|transmits a disease-causing agent between hosts|is itself always the pathogen|provides a medical diagnosis|makes water safe|A vector transmits an infectious agent; the pathogen and vector are distinct.
A clean-looking food|may still contain harmful microorganisms|is guaranteed sterile|never needs safe storage|can never cause illness|Appearance does not establish food safety.
A qualified professional can advise about contraception because|methods differ in suitability and individual circumstances|all methods suit everyone equally|all methods prevent STIs equally|appearance establishes pregnancy|Contraceptive options differ and should be considered in context.
A person with sustained undetectable HIV viral load|does not sexually transmit HIV according to WHO guidance|is automatically cured|can transmit HIV by hugging|never needs follow-up care|WHO states that people with a sustained undetectable viral load do not sexually transmit HIV.
A suspected embedded object in a bleeding wound should|be left in place while pressure is applied around it and help is sought|be pulled out immediately|be pressed deeper|be ignored without seeking care|Removing an embedded object can worsen bleeding; seek help and apply pressure around it.
A health risk factor|does not by itself explain every individual case|proves that every exposed person will become ill|is the same as a diagnosis|eliminates all environmental influences|Risk factors affect probability and do not prove individual causation.
A safer waste system includes|separation, collection and appropriate disposal|open dumping beside a water source|burning all waste without safeguards|handling discarded needles with bare hands|Waste needs appropriate separation, collection, treatment and disposal.
A health textbook is|educational and not a substitute for individual clinical care|a complete diagnosis for any symptom|an emergency service|a universal prescription|Study material supports learning but does not replace individual assessment or emergency care.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
